A worked publication page. It sets its own `author:`, a `date:`, and a
`bibliography:`, so the **Cite this** box renders with this page's authors as the
byline and a scholarly (`article-journal` / `JOUR`) type.

The `author:` here is the **structured** form: each entry carries a name plus the
things a byline, a Google Scholar `citation_*` tag and a JSON-LD `Person` all need
to say about a person. Affiliations are written as names rather than as indices
into a separate list, and the superscript numbers a reader sees are derived from
first appearance — so the two authors here share the number `1` because they typed
the same institution, with no index to keep in sync. The scalar spelling
(`author: "Grace Hopper"`) stays valid and is what every other page in this corpus
still uses; `note.tmd` next door exercises the site-level fallback.

Under the byline sit the `venue:`/`award:` badges and the `links:` **resource
row**. Two of those three links are written as bare URLs: the label and the icon
are read off the host, so "arXiv" and "Code" appear nowhere in this page's source.
The third uses the `{text:, href:}` map form, which is what the inference is *not*
required to guess. The same declaration feeds the metadata — `venue:` becomes
`citation_conference_title`, and the arXiv id in `citation_arxiv_id` is derived
from that first link rather than declared a second time.

After the references, the generated **appendix** collects the three things a paper
owes a reader that prose tends to forget: who did what (`contribution:`, declared
beside each name rather than in a separate list that could drift out of sync with
it), who made it possible (`acknowledgments:`), and where the citable record lives
(`doi:`, written here in its `https://doi.org/…` spelling and normalised to the
bare identifier). Nothing in it reads a clock, so a rebuild of this page is
byte-identical.

The engine's design builds on earlier notes [@lovelace1843].
